    {
      "id": "k_pneumoniae",
      "name": "Klebsiella pneumoniae",
      "group": "Gram-negative",
      "morphology": "Gram-negative encapsulated bacilli, non-motile",
      "tagline": "Encapsulated Enterobacterales causing healthcare-associated pneumonia, urinary and bloodstream infection, and hypervirulent liver abscess; carbapenem resistance is a global threat.",
      "diseases": [
        "Hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia",
        "Urinary tract infection",
        "Bacteraemia and sepsis",
        "Pyogenic liver abscess (hypervirulent strains)",
        "Pneumonia in alcohol use disorder and diabetes",
        "Neonatal sepsis"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Gram-negative, non-motile, encapsulated bacilli; lactose fermenter with mucoid colonies.\n• Oxidase-negative, urease-positive, indole-negative (distinguishes it from E. coli)."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Thick polysaccharide capsule resists phagocytosis and complement.\n• Hypermucoviscous phenotype in hypervirulent strains (positive string test).\n• Siderophores (enterobactin, and yersiniabactin or aerobactin in hypervirulent strains).\n• Type 1 and type 3 fimbriae support adhesion and biofilm formation.\n• Lipopolysaccharide endotoxin."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Colonizes the gut and, in hospital, the hands and equipment around ventilated and catheterized patients.\n• A leading cause of healthcare-associated pneumonia, urinary and bloodstream infection, especially in intensive care and neonatal units.\n• Hypervirulent lineages emerged in East Asia and are now reported worldwide.\n• Carbapenem-resistant strains are endemic in parts of Southern Europe, South Asia, Latin America and the United States, with KPC and NDM varying by region."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Classic community pneumonia: upper-lobe consolidation with 'currant jelly' sputum and cavitation, mostly in alcohol use disorder and diabetes.\n• Hospital: ventilator-associated pneumonia, catheter UTI, line infection.\n• Hypervirulent strains: community liver abscess, with metastatic endophthalmitis or meningitis, seen especially in East Asia and in people with diabetes."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Culture of sputum, urine, blood or aspirates with susceptibility testing.\n• Phenotypic ESBL and carbapenemase detection, and molecular tests for KPC, NDM and OXA-48.\n• Imaging for liver abscess; always assess for metastatic foci."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Intrinsic resistance to ampicillin (chromosomal SHV beta-lactamase).\n• ESBL producers: carbapenem.\n• KPC producers: ceftazidime-avibactam, meropenem-vaborbactam or imipenem-relebactam.\n• Metallo-beta-lactamase (NDM) producers: ceftazidime-avibactam with aztreonam, or cefiderocol.\n• Polymyxins and tigecycline are last-line options with major limits.\n• Liver abscess needs drainage as well as antibiotics."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Intrinsic: ampicillin and amoxicillin.\n• Acquired: ESBLs, AmpC, carbapenemases (KPC, NDM, OXA-48), and colistin resistance.\n• Hypervirulent strains were historically susceptible but convergence with resistance is now reported."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Contact precautions and screening of high-risk patients.\n• Antibiotic stewardship, especially carbapenem sparing.\n• Bundles for ventilator and catheter care."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Mucoid lactose-fermenting colonies with a positive string test point to hypervirulent K. pneumoniae.",
        "Klebsiella liver abscess: always look for eye and central nervous system spread.",
        "Carbapenem choice depends on the carbapenemase type, so identify it."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "IDSA guidance on treatment of antimicrobial-resistant gram-negative infections (current version)",
        "Local antimicrobial guidelines and susceptibility data"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n_meningitidis",
      "name": "Neisseria meningitidis",
      "group": "Gram-negative",
      "morphology": "Gram-negative diplococci (kidney-bean shaped)",
      "tagline": "Encapsulated diplococcus causing meningitis and rapidly fatal meningococcaemia, with outbreaks in adolescents, close-living groups and the Hajj.",
      "diseases": [
        "Acute bacterial meningitis",
        "Meningococcaemia with purpuric rash",
        "Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome (adrenal haemorrhage)",
        "Septic arthritis",
        "Pneumonia and conjunctivitis (rare)"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Gram-negative diplococci, oxidase-positive and catalase-positive.\n• Grows on chocolate or Thayer-Martin agar in CO2.\n• Ferments glucose and maltose (N. gonorrhoeae ferments glucose only).\n• Carried in the nasopharynx of about 10% of healthy people."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Polysaccharide capsule (serogroups A, B, C, W, Y, X) resists phagocytosis.\n• Lipooligosaccharide (LOS) endotoxin drives shock, DIC and purpura.\n• Pili mediate attachment; IgA protease cleaves mucosal IgA.\n• Factor H binding protein evades complement."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Humans are the only reservoir; carried in the nasopharynx by about 10% of people, with the highest carriage in adolescents and young adults.\n• Spread by respiratory droplets and close, prolonged contact (households, dormitories, military barracks).\n• Disease peaks in infants and in adolescents aged about 16 to 23, in late winter and early spring.\n• The African meningitis belt has epidemic disease in the dry season; serogroup distribution varies (B and C in Europe and the Americas, W and X in parts of Africa, W associated with the Hajj)."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Meningitis: fever, headache, neck stiffness, photophobia, altered mental state.\n• Meningococcaemia: abrupt fever, petechial to purpuric rash, hypotension and shock; may occur without meningitis.\n• Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome: bilateral adrenal haemorrhage with refractory shock.\n• Higher risk with terminal complement deficiency (C5 to C9), asplenia, and crowded living."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Lumbar puncture if no contraindication: CSF Gram stain, culture, cell count, glucose and protein.\n• Blood cultures before antibiotics, but never delay treatment for them.\n• PCR on CSF or blood; scrape of purpuric lesions may show diplococci.\n• Test for complement deficiency after recurrent or unusual-serogroup disease."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Empirical therapy is a third-generation cephalosporin (ceftriaxone or cefotaxime), started immediately.\n• Penicillin G is an option once susceptibility is confirmed.\n• Adjunctive dexamethasone is given with suspected bacterial meningitis per guidelines.\n• Intensive care for shock, with fluids and vasopressors.\n• Droplet precautions for the first 24 hours of effective therapy."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prophylaxis and Vaccines",
          "text": "• Close contacts receive a single dose of ciprofloxacin, or rifampin, or ceftriaxone (choice depends on age and pregnancy).\n• Quadrivalent conjugate vaccine (MenACWY) for adolescents, asplenia, complement deficiency, and pilgrims to the Hajj.\n• Serogroup B vaccines (4CMenB, MenB-FHbp) for at-risk groups and outbreaks."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Reduced penicillin susceptibility occurs in some regions.\n• Ciprofloxacin-resistant isolates have been reported, so check local patterns.\n• Ceftriaxone resistance is very rare."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Petechial rash with fever is a meningococcal emergency: give antibiotics before imaging or lumbar puncture if delayed.",
        "Recurrent meningococcal disease suggests terminal complement deficiency.",
        "Close contacts need prophylaxis the same day."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "Tunkel AR et al. IDSA guidelines for bacterial meningitis. Clin Infect Dis 2004",
        "Local public health guidance on contact prophylaxis"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h_influenzae",
      "name": "Haemophilus influenzae",
      "group": "Gram-negative",
      "morphology": "Gram-negative pleomorphic coccobacilli",
      "tagline": "Fastidious coccobacillus: encapsulated type b caused childhood meningitis and epiglottitis before vaccination, while non-typeable strains drive otitis media and COPD exacerbations.",
      "diseases": [
        "Meningitis (type b)",
        "Epiglottitis (type b)",
        "Pneumonia and bacteraemia",
        "Septic arthritis in young children",
        "Acute otitis media and sinusitis (non-typeable)",
        "COPD exacerbation (non-typeable)"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Small Gram-negative coccobacilli, facultative anaerobe.\n• Needs factor X (haemin) and factor V (NAD): grows on chocolate agar, not on plain blood agar.\n• Shows satellite growth around S. aureus on blood agar.\n• Six capsular types (a to f); type b has the PRP capsule, and non-typeable strains are unencapsulated."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Type b capsule (polyribosyl ribitol phosphate) prevents phagocytosis.\n• IgA protease cleaves secretory IgA.\n• Pili and adhesins for mucosal colonization; lipooligosaccharide endotoxin.\n• Biofilm formation drives chronic and recurrent infection."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Humans are the only reservoir; non-typeable strains colonize the nasopharynx of most healthy people.\n• Type b disease has fallen dramatically where the conjugate vaccine is routine, but it persists where vaccine coverage is low.\n• Invasive non-typeable disease is now the dominant form in vaccinated populations, mostly in older adults, neonates and people with chronic lung disease.\n• Spread by respiratory droplets and direct contact."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Hib meningitis: fever, neck stiffness, irritability in unvaccinated children; sensorineural hearing loss is a classic complication.\n• Epiglottitis: drooling, muffled voice, tripod posture and stridor; the airway can close suddenly.\n• Non-typeable: otitis media, sinusitis, bronchitis, and pneumonia in COPD."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Gram stain and culture on chocolate agar; X and V factor requirement confirms identity.\n• Blood and CSF cultures in invasive disease; PCR and capsular typing where available.\n• Epiglottitis: secure the airway first; lateral neck X-ray may show the thumb sign, but never delay airway management."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Invasive disease: ceftriaxone or cefotaxime.\n• Otitis media or sinusitis: high-dose amoxicillin, or amoxicillin-clavulanate if beta-lactamase producers are likely.\n• Alternatives: doxycycline, or respiratory fluoroquinolones in adults.\n• Epiglottitis: airway protection by an experienced clinician plus ceftriaxone.\n• Dexamethasone adjunct is considered in childhood Hib meningitis."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Beta-lactamase production (TEM-1) in a substantial proportion of isolates.\n• BLNAR strains have altered PBP3 and resist ampicillin without a beta-lactamase.\n• Check local susceptibility."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Hib conjugate vaccine in the routine childhood schedule, which has nearly eliminated type b disease.\n• Rifampin prophylaxis for household contacts when an unvaccinated child under 4 is present.\n• No vaccine yet for non-typeable strains."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Chocolate agar plus X and V factors and satellitism identify H. influenzae.",
        "Epiglottitis is now rare but remains an airway emergency.",
        "Hib vaccine cut invasive disease in children by over 90%."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "Local childhood immunisation and antimicrobial guidelines",
        "Tunkel AR et al. IDSA guidelines for bacterial meningitis. Clin Infect Dis 2004"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "salmonella",
      "name": "Salmonella enterica",
      "group": "Gram-negative",
      "morphology": "Gram-negative bacilli, motile, non-lactose fermenters",
      "tagline": "Typhoidal serovars cause enteric fever; non-typhoidal serovars cause food-borne gastroenteritis and invasive disease in vulnerable hosts.",
      "diseases": [
        "Typhoid and paratyphoid (enteric) fever",
        "Non-typhoidal gastroenteritis",
        "Bacteraemia in immunocompromised patients",
        "Osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease",
        "Chronic gallbladder carriage"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Gram-negative bacilli; motile; non-lactose fermenting colonies on MacConkey.\n• Produces H2S: black colonies on Salmonella-Shigella and XLD agar.\n• Typhoidal serovars (Typhi, Paratyphi A, B, C) infect only humans; non-typhoidal serovars (Enteritidis, Typhimurium) have animal reservoirs."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Vi capsular antigen of S. Typhi resists phagocytosis and complement.\n• Type III secretion systems (SPI-1 and SPI-2) invade M cells and survive in macrophages.\n• Endotoxin and flagellar antigens (O, H and Vi)."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Typhoid and paratyphoid are spread by the faecal-oral route through contaminated food and water; humans are the only reservoir.\n• Typhoid burden is highest in South Asia and sub-Saharan Africa, with roughly 9 million cases and over 100,000 deaths a year.\n• Non-typhoidal serovars come from poultry, eggs, meat, fresh produce and reptiles, causing food-borne outbreaks worldwide.\n• In sub-Saharan Africa, invasive non-typhoidal disease affects children with malaria or malnutrition and adults with HIV."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Typhoid: stepwise rising fever, headache, relative bradycardia, constipation then diarrhoea, rose spots, hepatosplenomegaly.\n• Complications in week 3: intestinal bleeding and perforation.\n• Non-typhoidal: diarrhoea, fever and cramps for 3 to 7 days; spreads to blood in infants, elderly, HIV and sickle cell disease.\n• Sickle cell disease predisposes to Salmonella osteomyelitis.\n• Some patients become chronic gallbladder carriers."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Blood culture is positive in the first week; bone marrow culture is the most sensitive.\n• Stool culture becomes positive later; urine and duodenal samples are options.\n• Widal test is unreliable and should not be used alone.\n• Test for HIV and haemoglobinopathies in recurrent or invasive non-typhoidal disease."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Typhoid: azithromycin, ceftriaxone or a fluoroquinolone, chosen by the region of acquisition.\n• Fluoroquinolone resistance is very common for South Asian isolates; extensively drug-resistant typhoid (Pakistan) needs azithromycin or a carbapenem.\n• Non-typhoidal gastroenteritis in healthy people: oral rehydration, no antibiotics.\n• Antibiotics for non-typhoidal infection in infants, older adults, immunocompromised patients, sickle cell disease or invasive disease.\n• Chronic carriers: prolonged antibiotic therapy and sometimes cholecystectomy."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Multidrug-resistant typhoid (ampicillin, chloramphenicol, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole).\n• Fluoroquinolone resistance (gyrA mutations) and extended-spectrum cephalosporin resistance are increasing.\n• Check regional patterns before choosing empirical therapy."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Safe water, sanitation and food hygiene; thorough cooking of poultry and eggs.\n• Typhoid vaccines: Vi polysaccharide, oral Ty21a, and typhoid conjugate vaccine.\n• Avoid contact with reptiles and poultry in high-risk groups; exclude carriers from food handling."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Rose spots, relative bradycardia and leucopenia suggest typhoid.",
        "Do not treat uncomplicated non-typhoidal gastroenteritis with antibiotics in healthy adults.",
        "Sickle cell disease leads to Salmonella osteomyelitis."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "WHO guidance on typhoid vaccines and treatment",
        "Local antimicrobial guidelines and regional susceptibility data"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_difficile",
      "name": "Clostridioides difficile",
      "group": "Anaerobe",
      "morphology": "Gram-positive spore-forming anaerobic bacilli",
      "tagline": "Toxin-producing anaerobe and the leading cause of healthcare-associated antibiotic-related diarrhoea, ranging from mild colitis to fulminant pseudomembranous colitis.",
      "diseases": [
        "Antibiotic-associated diarrhoea",
        "Pseudomembranous colitis",
        "Fulminant colitis with toxic megacolon",
        "Recurrent C. difficile infection"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Gram-positive, strictly anaerobic, spore-forming bacilli.\n• Spores survive alcohol, heat and many disinfectants, and persist on hospital surfaces for months.\n• Grows on selective cycloserine-cefoxitin-fructose agar with a characteristic horse-manure odour.\n• Colonizes the gut of some healthy adults and many infants without disease."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Toxin A (TcdA, enterotoxin) and toxin B (TcdB, cytotoxin) glucosylate Rho GTPases, causing cell rounding, inflammation and death.\n• Binary toxin (CDT) in some strains adds virulence.\n• Hypervirulent NAP1/BI/027 strain: tcdC deletion causes toxin overproduction, and it is fluoroquinolone-resistant.\n• Spore formation drives transmission and relapse."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• The most common cause of healthcare-associated infectious diarrhoea in high-income countries.\n• Community-onset cases account for a growing share, often after outpatient antibiotics.\n• Risk rises with age over 65, long hospital stays, antibiotic exposure, and acid suppression.\n• Transmission is by the faecal-oral route via spores on hands, surfaces and equipment; hypervirulent ribotype 027 caused large epidemics in North America and Europe in the 2000s."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Watery diarrhoea, lower abdominal pain, fever and leucocytosis.\n• Pseudomembranous colitis: yellow-white plaques seen at colonoscopy.\n• Fulminant disease: ileus, toxic megacolon, hypotension, very high white cell count, rising creatinine; diarrhoea may stop when the colon dilates.\n• Risk factors: recent antibiotics (clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, cephalosporins, carbapenems), hospitalization, age over 65, proton pump inhibitors, inflammatory bowel disease and immunosuppression.\n• About 20% relapse after a first episode."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Test only unformed stool (3 or more loose stools in 24 hours) from patients with symptoms.\n• Two-step algorithm: glutamate dehydrogenase plus toxin immunoassay, with nucleic acid amplification (PCR) to settle disagreements.\n• PCR alone detects colonization and can overcall disease.\n• Do not test for cure and do not repeat tests within 7 days."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Stop the inciting antibiotic as soon as possible and review proton pump inhibitor use.\n• First episode: fidaxomicin is preferred; oral vancomycin is an acceptable alternative.\n• Metronidazole is only for non-severe disease when the first-line drugs are unavailable.\n• Fulminant disease: oral (high dose) vancomycin plus intravenous metronidazole, rectal vancomycin if ileus is present, and early surgical review for colectomy.\n• Recurrence: fidaxomicin or a tapered-pulsed vancomycin regimen; faecal microbiota transplantation or approved microbiota-based products for multiple recurrences.\n• Avoid antimotility drugs in active colitis."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Fluoroquinolone resistance in epidemic ribotype 027 favours its spread.\n• Reduced metronidazole susceptibility has been reported in some regions.\n• Vancomycin and fidaxomicin resistance is rare."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Antibiotic stewardship is the most effective measure.\n• Contact precautions with a private room and gloves and gowns.\n• Wash hands with soap and water, because alcohol gel does not kill spores.\n• Sporicidal environmental cleaning, such as chlorine-based agents.\n• Isolate until at least 48 hours after diarrhoea stops, per local policy."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Alcohol hand gel does not kill C. difficile spores: use soap and water.",
        "Do not test formed stool or test for cure.",
        "Fidaxomicin lowers recurrence compared with vancomycin."
      ],
      "references": [
        "McDonald LC et al. IDSA/SHEA clinical practice guidelines for C. difficile infection (2017 update and 2021 focused update). Clin Infect Dis",
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "Local infection prevention policy"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "legionella",
      "name": "Legionella pneumophila",
      "group": "Atypical",
      "morphology": "Gram-negative aerobic bacilli, poorly staining",
      "tagline": "Waterborne intracellular pathogen causing Legionnaires' disease, a severe atypical pneumonia with extrapulmonary features, and mild Pontiac fever.",
      "diseases": [
        "Legionnaires' disease (severe pneumonia)",
        "Pontiac fever (self-limited flu-like illness)",
        "Hospital-acquired pneumonia from contaminated water",
        "Extrapulmonary infection (rare)"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Thin Gram-negative bacilli that stain poorly with Gram stain; visualized by silver stain (Dieterle) or direct fluorescent antibody.\n• Aerobic, facultative intracellular organism of freshwater, growing inside amoebae and biofilms.\n• Needs buffered charcoal yeast extract (BCYE) agar with iron and L-cysteine; does not grow on routine media.\n• Serogroup 1 of L. pneumophila causes most disease."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Dot/Icm type IV secretion system delivers effectors that create a replicative vacuole inside macrophages.\n• Survives and multiplies in alveolar macrophages and in free-living amoebae.\n• Flagella and pili aid colonization of lung tissue; biofilm formation protects the organism in water systems."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Found in natural freshwater and in man-made water systems such as cooling towers, hot water systems and spa pools.\n• Cases are sporadic or cluster in outbreaks, with more cases in summer and early autumn.\n• Incidence has been rising in North America and Europe; case fatality is about 5 to 10% overall, higher in hospital-acquired disease.\n• Travel-associated and healthcare-associated clusters are common."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Transmission by inhaling aerosolized water (cooling towers, showers, hot tubs, humidifiers, decorative fountains); no person-to-person spread.\n• Pneumonia with high fever, dry cough, dyspnoea, headache and confusion.\n• Extrapulmonary clues: diarrhoea, relative bradycardia, hyponatraemia, raised liver enzymes and creatine kinase, haematuria.\n• Risk factors: age over 50, smoking, chronic lung disease, immunosuppression, recent travel or hotel stay, and healthcare exposure.\n• Pontiac fever: self-limited fever and myalgia without pneumonia after a high-dose exposure."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Urinary antigen test is fast but detects only L. pneumophila serogroup 1.\n• Lower respiratory culture on BCYE agar or PCR (best sensitivity and covers other species and serogroups) in suspected cases.\n• Gram stain shows many neutrophils but few or no bacteria.\n• Chest imaging shows patchy to lobar consolidation that can progress despite beta-lactam treatment.\n• Report confirmed cases to public health for source investigation."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Respiratory fluoroquinolones (levofloxacin or moxifloxacin) or azithromycin, which penetrate cells.\n• Beta-lactams, including cephalosporins, and aminoglycosides do not work against intracellular organisms.\n• Severe or immunocompromised cases: consider fluoroquinolone with or without a macrolide, per local guidance.\n• Treatment duration is typically 7 to 10 days, longer in severe or immunosuppressed cases.\n• Hospital water systems need remediation when a case is acquired there."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Intrinsically unresponsive to beta-lactams in practice because the organism lives inside cells.\n• Acquired resistance to macrolides and fluoroquinolones is rare, but treatment failure should prompt culture and susceptibility review.\n• Empirical cover for atypicals in community pneumonia protocols includes Legionella."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Water system management plans: temperature control, disinfection and flushing of low-use outlets.\n• Regular maintenance of cooling towers, hot tubs and decorative fountains.\n• No vaccine."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Pneumonia plus diarrhoea, hyponatraemia and relative bradycardia should prompt Legionella testing.",
        "Urinary antigen misses non-serogroup 1 and non-pneumophila species, so send PCR or culture too.",
        "Beta-lactams fail: use a fluoroquinolone or azithromycin."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "Metlay JP et al. ATS/IDSA guideline for community-acquired pneumonia. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2019",
        "Local public health guidance on Legionella control"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "m_pneumoniae",
      "name": "Mycoplasma pneumoniae",
      "group": "Atypical",
      "morphology": "Pleomorphic bacteria lacking a cell wall",
      "tagline": "Smallest free-living bacterium, causing 'walking pneumonia' and tracheobronchitis in older children and young adults, with notable extrapulmonary complications.",
      "diseases": [
        "Atypical ('walking') pneumonia",
        "Tracheobronchitis",
        "Cold agglutinin haemolytic anaemia",
        "Mycoplasma-induced rash and mucositis, Stevens-Johnson-like syndrome",
        "Neurological complications (encephalitis, Guillain-Barre syndrome)",
        "Bullous myringitis"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• No cell wall: not visible on Gram stain and intrinsically resistant to beta-lactams.\n• Membrane contains cholesterol (taken from the host); grows slowly on specialised media such as SP4, which makes culture impractical.\n• Attaches to respiratory epithelium and remains extracellular."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• P1 adhesin binds sialylated receptors on respiratory epithelium and damages cilia.\n• Community-acquired respiratory distress syndrome (CARDS) toxin.\n• Hydrogen peroxide and superoxide production injure host cells; immune-mediated injury drives much of the illness.\n• Molecular mimicry with the I antigen on red cells produces cold agglutinins."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Accounts for roughly 15 to 20% of community-acquired pneumonia, with epidemics every 3 to 7 years.\n• Most common in school-age children and young adults; spread by respiratory droplets with an incubation of 1 to 3 weeks.\n• Outbreaks occur in schools, households, military camps and other crowded settings.\n• Cases fell during COVID-19 restrictions and rebounded afterwards."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Gradual onset of low-grade fever, headache, malaise, sore throat and persistent dry cough over 1 to 3 weeks.\n• Chest X-ray often looks worse than the patient (patchy or reticulonodular infiltrates).\n• Extrapulmonary: haemolytic anaemia with cold agglutinins (IgM), erythema multiforme and mucositis, myocarditis, meningoencephalitis, Guillain-Barre syndrome and arthralgia.\n• Peaks in school-age children and young adults, with outbreaks in crowded settings and epidemic cycles every few years."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• PCR of throat or nasopharyngeal swab is the preferred test.\n• Serology (IgM, or a rise in IgG paired samples) is helpful but slow.\n• Cold agglutinin titre is non-specific and supports the diagnosis only.\n• Gram stain and routine culture are unhelpful."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Macrolides (azithromycin) are first-line for children and adults; doxycycline is an alternative, and a respiratory fluoroquinolone is an option in adults.\n• Macrolide-resistant strains (23S rRNA mutations, common in parts of Asia) are treated with doxycycline or a fluoroquinolone, with specialist advice in children.\n• Beta-lactams and other cell-wall agents are ineffective.\n• Mild disease often resolves without treatment; treatment shortens symptoms.\n• Corticosteroids or immunomodulators are considered in severe immune-mediated complications, with specialist input."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Intrinsic resistance to all cell-wall active agents (beta-lactams, glycopeptides) and to sulfonamides.\n• Acquired macrolide resistance by point mutations in the 23S rRNA gene (such as A2063G), with prevalence varying greatly by country.\n• Fluoroquinolone and tetracycline resistance is rare but reported."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Droplet precautions and hand and respiratory hygiene.\n• Outbreak control in schools, military settings and dormitories.\n• No licensed vaccine."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Atypical pneumonia not responding to a beta-lactam: think Mycoplasma, Legionella or Chlamydia.",
        "Cold agglutinins and haemolysis point to Mycoplasma.",
        "Chest X-ray looks worse than the patient."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "Metlay JP et al. ATS/IDSA guideline for community-acquired pneumonia. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2019",
        "Local antimicrobial guidelines and resistance data"
      ]
