    {
      "id": "enterococcus",
      "name": "Enterococcus faecalis and faecium",
      "group": "Gram-positive",
      "morphology": "Gram-positive cocci in pairs and short chains",
      "tagline": "Hardy gut commensals that cause urinary, biliary, wound and bloodstream infection and endocarditis; intrinsic resistance and vancomycin resistance (VRE) make them a leading healthcare-associated problem.",
      "diseases": [
        "Urinary tract infection (often catheter-associated)",
        "Bacteraemia and endocarditis",
        "Intra-abdominal and biliary infection",
        "Wound and pressure ulcer infection",
        "Neonatal sepsis",
        "Device and line infection"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Gram-positive cocci in pairs and short chains; catalase-negative.\n• Facultative anaerobe that is PYR-positive, hydrolyses esculin in the presence of 40% bile, and grows in 6.5% NaCl.\n• Usually non- or alpha-haemolytic; E. faecalis causes most infections, but E. faecium is more often multidrug-resistant.\n• Lancefield group D antigen."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Aggregation substance and adhesins promote colonization and biofilm formation on catheters and valves.\n• Cytolysin (E. faecalis) is a haemolysin and bacteriocin.\n• Gelatinase and enterococcal surface protein (Esp) aid tissue invasion and biofilm.\n• Survives harsh conditions: high salt, bile, heat and many disinfectants.\n• Efficient gene transfer by conjugative plasmids and transposons spreads resistance."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Normal inhabitants of the gut, and sometimes the female genital tract and oral cavity.\n• Among the leading causes of healthcare-associated infection, especially in intensive care, haematology and transplant units.\n• Risk factors: prolonged hospital stay, previous cephalosporin or vancomycin use, urinary catheters, abdominal surgery and immunosuppression.\n• Spread on hands, equipment and surfaces; VRE prevalence varies widely by country and by hospital, and is highest for E. faecium."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Catheter-associated UTI and pyelonephritis are the commonest syndromes.\n• Bacteraemia from a urinary, biliary or intra-abdominal source or from an infected line.\n• Subacute endocarditis, especially in older men with a genitourinary source or in those with prosthetic valves.\n• Polymicrobial intra-abdominal infection and surgical wound infection.\n• Neonatal sepsis in preterm infants."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Gram stain and culture of urine, blood or tissue; identify to species level (E. faecalis versus E. faecium) and test susceptibility.\n• Bile-esculin, PYR and growth in 6.5% NaCl identify the genus.\n• Molecular van gene tests or phenotypic MICs classify VRE.\n• In enterococcal bacteraemia, search for endocarditis and drain any source, and repeat blood cultures."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Ampicillin-susceptible isolates (mainly E. faecalis): amoxicillin or ampicillin for urinary infection and bacteraemia.\n• Endocarditis: ampicillin plus ceftriaxone (double beta-lactam) is preferred for E. faecalis, with ampicillin plus gentamicin as an alternative where gentamicin is safe.\n• Ampicillin-resistant, vancomycin-susceptible strains: vancomycin.\n• VRE: linezolid or daptomycin at higher doses (check susceptibility and site); nitrofurantoin or fosfomycin for uncomplicated VRE cystitis.\n• Remove infected catheters and drain collections.\n• Cephalosporins, clindamycin and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole are not reliable: enterococci are intrinsically resistant or appear active in vitro but fail in vivo."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Intrinsic: all cephalosporins, clindamycin, low-level aminoglycoside resistance and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (in vivo).\n• Ampicillin resistance in E. faecium arises from altered PBP5 overproduction or mutation.\n• vanA: high-level resistance to vancomycin and teicoplanin; vanB: vancomycin resistance with teicoplanin susceptibility; vanC is intrinsic and low-level in E. gallinarum and E. casseliflavus.\n• Van genes change the cell wall precursor from D-Ala-D-Ala to D-Ala-D-Lac.\n• Linezolid resistance (23S rRNA mutations, cfr, optrA) and daptomycin resistance are emerging.\n• High-level gentamicin resistance rules out synergy with aminoglycosides."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Hand hygiene and contact precautions for VRE colonized or infected patients.\n• Environmental cleaning of high-touch surfaces and dedicated equipment.\n• Antibiotic stewardship, especially limiting cephalosporin and vancomycin use.\n• Remove urinary catheters and central lines as soon as they are not needed."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Cephalosporins do not cover enterococci, a classic cause of breakthrough bacteraemia.",
        "Ampicillin plus ceftriaxone works for E. faecalis endocarditis because of complementary PBP binding.",
        "Always identify to species: E. faecalis is usually ampicillin-susceptible, E. faecium usually is not."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "Baddour LM et al. AHA scientific statement on infective endocarditis in adults. Circulation 2015",
        "IDSA guidance on treatment of antimicrobial-resistant infections (current version)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "a_baumannii",
      "name": "Acinetobacter baumannii",
      "group": "Gram-negative",
      "morphology": "Gram-negative coccobacilli, strictly aerobic non-fermenter",
      "tagline": "Desiccation-tolerant, multidrug-resistant opportunist causing ventilator-associated pneumonia, bloodstream and wound infection in intensive care units; carbapenem resistance is now widespread.",
      "diseases": [
        "Ventilator-associated and hospital-acquired pneumonia",
        "Bloodstream and catheter-related infection",
        "Wound, burn and trauma infection",
        "Post-neurosurgical meningitis",
        "Catheter-associated urinary tract infection"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Gram-negative coccobacilli that may look like Gram-positive diplococci on smear.\n• Strictly aerobic, non-motile, non-lactose fermenting (pale colonies on MacConkey).\n• Oxidase-negative and catalase-positive, which separates it from Pseudomonas (oxidase-positive).\n• Survives for weeks on dry hospital surfaces."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Outer membrane protein A (OmpA) mediates adhesion and cell death.\n• Capsule, biofilm formation and desiccation tolerance support survival on surfaces and devices.\n• Efflux pumps and low outer membrane permeability reduce antibiotic entry.\n• Lipopolysaccharide and phospholipases; natural competence allows rapid acquisition of resistance genes."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Mainly a healthcare-associated pathogen in intensive care units and burn units.\n• Risk factors: mechanical ventilation, prolonged stay, previous broad-spectrum antibiotics, invasive devices and burns.\n• Gained notoriety in military wounded in Iraq and Afghanistan.\n• Carbapenem-resistant A. baumannii (CRAB) is endemic in many regions of Asia, the Middle East, Southern Europe and Latin America."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Ventilator-associated pneumonia is the commonest syndrome, with high mortality.\n• Bacteraemia from lines, the lungs or wounds.\n• Wound and burn infection; osteomyelitis after trauma.\n• Meningitis after neurosurgery or with external ventricular drains.\n• Isolation from sputum may represent colonization, so interpret together with the clinical picture."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Culture of sputum, blood, wound or CSF; identify to species with MALDI-TOF.\n• Susceptibility testing is essential because patterns vary between units.\n• Molecular detection of OXA carbapenemase genes where available.\n• Differentiate colonization from infection to avoid unnecessary treatment."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Carbapenem (meropenem or imipenem) if susceptible.\n• Carbapenem-resistant disease: high-dose ampicillin-sulbactam (sulbactam has intrinsic activity) combined with at least one other active agent, such as polymyxin B, minocycline or tigecycline, or cefiderocol.\n• Sulbactam-durlobactam is an option for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia.\n• Polymyxins have toxicity (kidney and nerve) and limited lung penetration; tigecycline is not suitable for bloodstream infection or pneumonia as monotherapy.\n• Source control and device removal are part of treatment.\n• Combination therapy is often used, with specialist infectious diseases advice."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Intrinsic: ampicillin, amoxicillin-clavulanate, first- and second-generation cephalosporins, ertapenem and chloramphenicol, among others.\n• OXA-23, OXA-24/40 and OXA-58 carbapenemases are most common; NDM and other metallo-beta-lactamases also occur.\n• Chromosomal AmpC (ADC), efflux pumps, porin loss and altered PBPs.\n• Colistin resistance via pmrAB mutations and lipid A modification.\n• Resistance genes cluster on mobile genetic islands."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Strict hand hygiene and contact precautions for CRAB.\n• Environmental cleaning with attention to high-touch surfaces and shared equipment.\n• Cohorting of colonized and infected patients and screening in outbreaks.\n• Antibiotic stewardship, particularly limiting carbapenem use, and care bundles for ventilators and catheters."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Oxidase-negative Gram-negative coccobacilli in a ventilated patient: think Acinetobacter.",
        "Sulbactam is active on its own against Acinetobacter, which is why it is used at high doses.",
        "Colonization is common: treat infection, not a positive culture."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "IDSA guidance on the treatment of antimicrobial-resistant gram-negative infections (current version)",
        "Local antimicrobial guidelines and susceptibility data"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "v_cholerae",
      "name": "Vibrio cholerae",
      "group": "Gram-negative",
      "morphology": "Curved Gram-negative bacilli (comma-shaped) with a single polar flagellum",
      "tagline": "Toxigenic waterborne pathogen causing profuse rice-water diarrhoea and rapid dehydration; epidemics follow poor sanitation and unsafe water.",
      "diseases": [
        "Cholera (profuse secretory diarrhoea)",
        "Severe dehydration with hypovolaemic shock",
        "Electrolyte disturbance (hypokalaemia, metabolic acidosis)",
        "Non-O1 and non-O139 gastroenteritis and wound infection (rare)"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Curved Gram-negative bacilli, highly motile with a single polar flagellum.\n• Oxidase-positive; ferments glucose and sucrose, so it makes yellow colonies on TCBS agar.\n• Tolerates alkaline conditions (alkaline peptone water enrichment) and is killed by gastric acid.\n• O1 (classical and El Tor biotypes) and O139 serogroups cause epidemic cholera."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Cholera toxin (encoded by the CTX phage) is an AB5 toxin: the A subunit ADP-ribosylates Gs, raising cAMP and driving chloride and water secretion.\n• Toxin-coregulated pilus (TCP) mediates colonization and is the receptor for CTX phage.\n• Mucinase, neuraminidase and flagellar motility help penetrate the mucus layer.\n• Non-invasive: the organism stays in the lumen, so there is no blood or pus in the stool."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Transmitted through contaminated water and food (including raw shellfish); outbreaks follow floods, conflict and displacement.\n• Endemic in South Asia, sub-Saharan Africa and parts of the Middle East; the seventh pandemic (El Tor) began in 1961.\n• Major outbreaks: Haiti (2010 onwards) and Yemen (2016 onwards).\n• Estimated 1.3 to 4 million cases and 21,000 to 143,000 deaths per year worldwide.\n• Most infections are mild or asymptomatic, but they can still spread the organism."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Incubation from a few hours to 5 days.\n• Sudden painless watery diarrhoea, 'rice-water' stool, with vomiting; fluid loss can reach 1 litre per hour.\n• Dehydration: sunken eyes, absent skin turgor, thirst, oliguria, hypotension and shock.\n• Hypokalaemia (cramps, arrhythmia) and metabolic acidosis from bicarbonate loss.\n• Without treatment, death can occur within hours; with prompt rehydration, mortality is under 1%."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Clinical diagnosis in an outbreak setting; start rehydration immediately.\n• Stool or rectal swab culture on TCBS agar; dark-field microscopy shows darting motility.\n• Rapid dipstick tests for O1 and O139 antigen are useful for field surveillance.\n• PCR for the ctxA gene confirms toxigenic strains.\n• Report suspected cases to public health authorities."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Rehydration is the lifesaving treatment: oral rehydration solution for mild and moderate dehydration, and intravenous Ringer's lactate for severe dehydration.\n• Monitor fluid balance and replace ongoing losses; correct potassium and acidosis.\n• Antibiotics shorten illness and reduce stool volume in moderate to severe cases: single-dose azithromycin (or doxycycline in non-pregnant adults) is commonly used; follow local resistance patterns.\n• Zinc supplementation for children.\n• Continue feeding, and breastfeeding in infants."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Tetracycline resistance and multidrug-resistant strains carrying the SXT integrating conjugative element (trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, streptomycin, chloramphenicol) are reported in many regions.\n• Reduced ciprofloxacin susceptibility is common in El Tor strains.\n• Azithromycin remains active in most areas, but check local surveillance."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Safe drinking water, sanitation and handwashing.\n• Oral cholera vaccines (killed, two doses, or a single-dose live vaccine for travellers) are used in endemic areas, outbreaks and for travellers at risk.\n• Thorough cooking of seafood and food safety.\n• Household and community measures during outbreaks, plus targeted chemoprophylaxis for close contacts where recommended."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Treat the dehydration first: rehydration saves lives, and antibiotics are an adjunct.",
        "Rice-water stool without blood or pus is classic for cholera.",
        "Cholera toxin acts by raising cAMP, similar in effect to ETEC's heat-labile toxin."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "WHO guidance on cholera case management and oral cholera vaccines",
        "Local public health guidance"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "t_pallidum",
      "name": "Treponema pallidum",
      "group": "Atypical",
      "morphology": "Thin, tightly coiled spirochete (too thin for Gram stain)",
      "tagline": "Spirochete causing syphilis, the great imitator: a staged disease from painless chancre to late cardiovascular and neurological damage, also transmitted from mother to fetus.",
      "diseases": [
        "Primary syphilis (painless chancre)",
        "Secondary syphilis (rash, condylomata lata)",
        "Latent syphilis",
        "Tertiary syphilis (gummas, aortitis)",
        "Neurosyphilis (tabes dorsalis, general paresis)",
        "Ocular and otic syphilis",
        "Congenital syphilis"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Spirochete with a tight helical coil; too thin to see on Gram stain.\n• Seen by dark-field microscopy, direct fluorescent antibody or silver stains (Warthin-Starry).\n• Cannot be grown on routine culture media; very slow dividing (about 30 hours).\n• Fragile outside the host: killed by drying, heat and many disinfectants."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Few surface-exposed outer membrane proteins, which helps it evade antibody (stealth pathogen).\n• Antigenic variation of the TprK protein allows immune escape.\n• Corkscrew motility from periplasmic endoflagella lets it cross tissues and mucosa.\n• Hyaluronidase and adhesins promote spread and attachment.\n• Lipoproteins trigger inflammation; endarteritis and perivascular plasma-cell infiltration cause much of the tissue damage."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Spread mainly by sexual contact through mucosal or skin lesions, and across the placenta (at any stage of pregnancy); rarely by blood transfusion.\n• Humans are the only reservoir.\n• Rising incidence in many countries, particularly in men who have sex with men, with growing rates among heterosexual adults and in congenital syphilis.\n• HIV co-infection is common, and each infection makes the other more likely."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Primary: a single painless, indurated ulcer (chancre) at the site of inoculation about 3 weeks after exposure, with painless regional lymph nodes; heals by itself within weeks.\n• Secondary (weeks to months later): diffuse maculopapular rash, often involving the palms and soles, mucous patches, condylomata lata, generalized lymphadenopathy, fever, malaise and patchy alopecia.\n• Latent: no symptoms but positive serology; early latent is under 1 year from infection and late latent is more than 1 year or unknown.\n• Tertiary (years later): gummas, cardiovascular syphilis (aortitis, aortic aneurysm and regurgitation) and neurosyphilis.\n• Neurosyphilis can occur at any stage: meningitis, stroke, tabes dorsalis (ataxia, lightning pains, Argyll Robertson pupil) and general paresis.\n• Congenital: stillbirth, rash, snuffles, hepatosplenomegaly, bone changes; late signs include Hutchinson teeth, interstitial keratitis, eighth nerve deafness and saddle nose."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Dark-field microscopy or PCR of fluid from a moist lesion in early disease.\n• Serology is the main tool: a nontreponemal test (RPR or VDRL) measures disease activity and titres, and a treponemal test (TPPA, FTA-ABS, EIA) confirms; use both.\n• Treponemal tests usually stay positive for life, while nontreponemal titres fall with successful treatment.\n• False-positive nontreponemal tests occur in pregnancy, lupus, antiphospholipid syndrome, viral infection and injection drug use; a very high titre can give a false negative (prozone effect).\n• CSF VDRL and cell count when neurological, eye or ear symptoms are present.\n• Test all patients for HIV and other sexually transmitted infections."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Penicillin is the treatment of choice for every stage.\n• Primary, secondary and early latent syphilis: a single intramuscular dose of benzathine penicillin G.\n• Late latent, unknown duration and tertiary syphilis without nervous system involvement: three weekly doses of benzathine penicillin G.\n• Neurosyphilis, ocular and otic syphilis: intravenous aqueous penicillin G for 10 to 14 days.\n• Pregnancy: penicillin only, with desensitization if allergic; congenital syphilis in newborns is treated with intravenous penicillin.\n• Penicillin-allergic non-pregnant adults: doxycycline for 14 days (early) or 28 days (late), after specialist advice.\n• Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction (fever, headache and myalgia within 24 hours of the first dose) is self-limited; warn patients.\n• Follow-up: repeat nontreponemal titres at 6 and 12 months, looking for at least a four-fold fall, and treat sexual partners."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• No penicillin resistance has ever been documented.\n• Macrolide resistance (23S rRNA mutations A2058G and A2059G) is widespread, so azithromycin should not be used.\n• Tetracycline resistance has not been reported."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Condoms and sexual health screening for people at risk.\n• Routine antenatal screening, and rescreening in the third trimester and at delivery in high-risk groups.\n• Partner notification and presumptive treatment of partners exposed within 90 days.\n• Doxycycline taken after sex has been shown to reduce syphilis in selected high-risk groups, and is used under national guidance.\n• No vaccine is available."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "A painless genital ulcer is syphilis until proven otherwise (chancroid is painful).",
        "Rash on the palms and soles: think secondary syphilis.",
        "Never use azithromycin for syphilis.",
        "Pregnant women with syphilis must receive penicillin."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines (current version)",
        "WHO guidelines on the treatment of syphilis"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_tetani",
      "name": "Clostridium tetani",
      "group": "Anaerobe",
      "morphology": "Gram-positive spore-forming anaerobic bacilli (terminal round spores)",
      "tagline": "Soil-dwelling anaerobe whose toxin, tetanospasmin, blocks inhibitory neurotransmitters and causes painful spasms, lockjaw and autonomic instability; fully preventable by vaccination.",
      "diseases": [
        "Generalized tetanus",
        "Localized tetanus",
        "Cephalic tetanus",
        "Neonatal tetanus"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Gram-positive, strictly anaerobic, motile bacilli.\n• Round terminal spores give a drumstick or tennis-racket appearance.\n• Spores live in soil, dust and animal faeces for years, and resist boiling and many disinfectants.\n• Grows in dead tissue with low oxygen, such as in puncture wounds and burns."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Tetanospasmin (tetanus toxin) is plasmid-encoded: a zinc metalloprotease that cleaves synaptobrevin (VAMP) in inhibitory interneurons.\n• The toxin travels by retrograde axonal transport to the spinal cord and brainstem.\n• Blocked release of GABA and glycine removes inhibition of motor neurons, causing rigidity and spasm.\n• Tetanolysin is a haemolysin; the bacteria stay in the wound and do not invade tissue."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Found worldwide, with most cases in places where vaccination coverage is low.\n• Neonatal tetanus follows contamination of the umbilical stump and still causes deaths in low-resource settings.\n• In high-income countries cases occur in older adults, people who inject drugs and unvaccinated individuals.\n• Incubation is usually 3 to 21 days; a shorter incubation and shorter time to the first spasm suggest more severe disease.\n• Not spread between people; disease does not give immunity, so patients still need vaccination."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Trismus (lockjaw) is the commonest first sign, followed by risus sardonicus and neck stiffness.\n• Opisthotonus, dysphagia, board-like abdominal rigidity and painful generalized spasms triggered by noise, light or touch, with preserved consciousness.\n• Autonomic instability: labile blood pressure, tachycardia, sweating and arrhythmias are a major cause of death in the second week.\n• Laryngospasm and respiratory failure; complications include fractures, aspiration pneumonia, rhabdomyolysis and thromboembolism.\n• Localized tetanus affects muscles near the wound; cephalic tetanus follows head wounds with cranial nerve palsies.\n• Neonatal tetanus: poor suckling, rigidity and spasms in the first weeks of life."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Diagnosis is clinical; do not wait for laboratory confirmation.\n• Wound culture is often negative and a positive culture does not prove disease.\n• Spatula test: touching the posterior pharynx triggers a masseter spasm (bite) instead of a gag reflex.\n• A serum antitoxin level above the protective threshold taken before immune globulin makes tetanus unlikely.\n• Look for the wound, but in many patients none is found."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Admit to intensive care, in a quiet, dark room with minimal stimulation.\n• Human tetanus immune globulin (given early) neutralizes circulating toxin but not toxin already bound in nerves.\n• Metronidazole is the preferred antibiotic; penicillin G is an alternative, although it may worsen spasms through GABA antagonism.\n• Wound cleaning and debridement.\n• Spasm control: benzodiazepines, magnesium sulfate, baclofen, and neuromuscular blockade with mechanical ventilation in severe cases; secure the airway early.\n• Autonomic instability: magnesium sulfate, sedation, and careful use of beta-blockers or clonidine.\n• Prevent complications with nutrition and thromboprophylaxis; recovery takes weeks as nerve terminals regrow.\n• Start the vaccine series during recovery."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Antibiotic resistance is not a clinical problem: metronidazole and penicillin remain active.\n• Antibiotics are secondary to toxin neutralization, wound care and supportive care."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Tetanus toxoid vaccination: the primary childhood series with boosters (Td or Tdap) about every 10 years.\n• Wound management: clean and debride; give a booster when the last dose was more than 10 years ago (5 years for dirty wounds).\n• Dirty wound and an unknown or incomplete vaccine history: give tetanus immune globulin as well as a vaccine dose.\n• Maternal immunization in pregnancy, clean delivery and cord care to prevent neonatal tetanus."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Tetanus toxin blocks inhibitory neurons, so the muscles fire without opposition.",
        "Disease does not give immunity: always vaccinate survivors.",
        "Trismus with a clear mind and spasms triggered by touch is tetanus."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "CDC Pink Book: Tetanus chapter",
        "WHO position paper on tetanus vaccines"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n_gonorrhoeae",
      "name": "Neisseria gonorrhoeae",
      "group": "Gram-negative",
      "morphology": "Gram-negative diplococci, typically inside neutrophils",
      "tagline": "Sexually transmitted diplococcus causing urethritis, cervicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease and disseminated infection; antibiotic resistance has narrowed treatment to few options.",
      "diseases": [
        "Urethritis and epididymitis",
        "Cervicitis and pelvic inflammatory disease",
        "Pharyngeal and rectal infection",
        "Disseminated gonococcal infection (arthritis-dermatitis syndrome)",
        "Neonatal conjunctivitis (ophthalmia neonatorum)",
        "Infertility and ectopic pregnancy"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Gram-negative diplococci with flattened adjacent sides (kidney-bean shape), often seen inside neutrophils.\n• Oxidase-positive and catalase-positive; ferments glucose only (N. meningitidis ferments glucose and maltose).\n• Fastidious: grows on chocolate agar or Thayer-Martin selective medium in 5% CO2.\n• No capsule, which distinguishes it from N. meningitidis."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Type IV pili mediate attachment and undergo antigenic variation, which is why no vaccine exists.\n• Opa proteins promote tight adhesion and invasion; porin proteins (PorB) interfere with phagocyte killing.\n• Lipooligosaccharide (LOS) endotoxin causes the intense neutrophil response and pus.\n• IgA protease cleaves mucosal IgA.\n• Evades complement by sialylating LOS and binding factor H."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Humans are the only host; spread by sexual contact and from mother to newborn during delivery.\n• WHO estimates over 80 million new infections a year in adults.\n• Highest rates are in sexually active young adults and men who have sex with men.\n• Co-infection with Chlamydia trachomatis is common, and gonorrhoea increases HIV transmission."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Men: purulent urethral discharge and dysuria within days of exposure; complications include epididymitis.\n• Women: often asymptomatic; cervicitis with discharge, then pelvic inflammatory disease, tubal scarring, infertility and ectopic pregnancy.\n• Pharyngeal and rectal infection are usually asymptomatic.\n• Disseminated gonococcal infection: fever, migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis and a pustular or vesicular rash, or purulent monoarthritis.\n• Neonatal conjunctivitis appears 2 to 5 days after birth with severe purulent discharge and can cause corneal perforation.\n• Women with complement deficiency (C5 to C9) are prone to recurrent disseminated disease."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) on first-catch urine or vaginal, cervical, urethral, pharyngeal or rectal swabs is the test of choice.\n• Gram stain of urethral discharge in symptomatic men shows intracellular Gram-negative diplococci and is diagnostic in that setting.\n• Culture on Thayer-Martin agar is still needed for susceptibility testing, particularly with treatment failure.\n• In suspected disseminated disease, culture blood, joint fluid and genital sites.\n• Always test for chlamydia, syphilis and HIV."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Ceftriaxone as a single intramuscular dose is the recommended first-line treatment for uncomplicated infection.\n• Add doxycycline when chlamydia has not been excluded.\n• If ceftriaxone cannot be used: gentamicin plus azithromycin, or other options per national guidance.\n• Disseminated infection and pelvic inflammatory disease need longer courses of ceftriaxone and specialist review.\n• Treat all sexual partners from the last 60 days, and advise abstinence until treatment is completed.\n• Retest after about 3 months, and perform a test of cure for pharyngeal infection and in treatment failure."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Resistance has developed to penicillin, tetracyclines and fluoroquinolones, so none of these can be used empirically.\n• Azithromycin resistance is rising, so it is no longer recommended as monotherapy or as dual therapy partner in many guidelines.\n• Ceftriaxone-resistant strains (for example the FC428 clone) have been reported in Asia, Europe and Australia, and extensively drug-resistant gonorrhoea is a global concern.\n• Culture and susceptibility testing is essential for any treatment failure.\n• New agents are in development."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Condoms and regular screening in those at risk.\n• Partner notification and treatment.\n• Eye prophylaxis for newborns where recommended.\n• No vaccine yet; the serogroup B meningococcal vaccine (4CMenB) gives partial cross-protection and is used in some national programmes."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Intracellular Gram-negative diplococci on a urethral smear are diagnostic in men.",
        "Think disseminated gonococcal infection with a sexually active young adult with migratory joint pain, tenosynovitis and pustules.",
        "Always treat for chlamydia as well unless it has been excluded."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines (current version)",
        "WHO guidelines on the treatment of Neisseria gonorrhoeae"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_trachomatis",
      "name": "Chlamydia trachomatis",
      "group": "Atypical",
      "morphology": "Obligate intracellular bacterium with elementary and reticulate body forms",
      "tagline": "Obligate intracellular pathogen causing the most common bacterial sexually transmitted infection, trachoma (leading infectious cause of blindness) and lymphogranuloma venereum.",
      "diseases": [
        "Urethritis, cervicitis and pelvic inflammatory disease",
        "Epididymitis and reactive arthritis",
        "Neonatal conjunctivitis and pneumonia",
        "Trachoma",
        "Lymphogranuloma venereum (LGV)",
        "Infertility and ectopic pregnancy"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Obligate intracellular bacterium that cannot grow on routine media or be seen reliably with a Gram stain.\n• Life cycle: the infectious elementary body (EB) enters the cell; it turns into a metabolically active reticulate body (RB) that divides inside an inclusion; RBs turn back into EBs and are released.\n• Cell wall lacks a conventional peptidoglycan layer; inclusions are seen with Giemsa or fluorescent antibody stains.\n• Serovars A to C cause trachoma, D to K cause genital and neonatal infection, and L1 to L3 cause LGV."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• The elementary body attaches to and is taken up by epithelial cells.\n• Inclusion membrane proteins prevent phagosome-lysosome fusion, creating a protected niche.\n• Type III secretion system injects effector proteins that remodel the host cell.\n• Persistent forms under immune or antibiotic pressure contribute to chronic scarring and reinfection.\n• Immune-mediated damage, not direct toxicity, causes most of the long-term injury."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• The most commonly reported bacterial sexually transmitted infection in many countries, with an estimated 120 million or more new infections a year worldwide.\n• Highest rates in sexually active adults under 25.\n• Trachoma is spread by flies, fingers and shared cloths in communities with poor water access and crowding.\n• LGV is endemic in tropical areas and has caused outbreaks of proctitis in men who have sex with men."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Often asymptomatic, especially in women.\n• Urethritis with mucoid discharge and dysuria; cervicitis; pelvic inflammatory disease with tubal damage; epididymitis.\n• Reactive arthritis (with urethritis and conjunctivitis) after genital infection.\n• Neonatal conjunctivitis 5 to 14 days after birth; infant pneumonia at 1 to 3 months with a staccato cough and no fever.\n• Trachoma: repeated follicular conjunctivitis, eyelid scarring, trichiasis (inturned lashes) and corneal opacity.\n• LGV: a transient painless ulcer, then painful inguinal lymph nodes (buboes) or haemorrhagic proctocolitis."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• NAAT on first-catch urine or vaginal, cervical or urethral swabs; rectal and pharyngeal swabs when exposed.\n• LGV-specific PCR identifies L serovars in rectal or bubo samples.\n• Screen sexually active women under 25, and others at risk, even without symptoms.\n• Culture needs cell lines and is not routine; serology has little value for genital infection.\n• Test for gonorrhoea, syphilis and HIV."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Doxycycline for 7 days is first-line for uncomplicated genital and rectal infection.\n• Azithromycin as a single dose is an alternative, and it is the usual choice in pregnancy.\n• LGV: doxycycline for 21 days.\n• Neonatal infection: oral macrolide (erythromycin or azithromycin).\n• Trachoma: single-dose azithromycin, with community mass treatment as part of the SAFE strategy (surgery for trichiasis, antibiotics, facial cleanliness, environmental improvement).\n• Treat all partners from the last 60 days, advise abstinence until treatment is completed, and retest at about 3 months."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Clinically significant resistance is rare.\n• Treatment failure occurs mostly from reinfection or non-adherence; doxycycline is more effective than azithromycin for rectal infection.\n• Beta-lactam antibiotics can induce persistent forms and are not used."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Condoms, screening programmes, and partner notification and treatment.\n• Eye prophylaxis for newborns where guidelines recommend it, and antenatal screening.\n• Trachoma control by the SAFE strategy.\n• No vaccine is available."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Gonorrhoea and chlamydia often occur together, so treat for both unless one is excluded.",
        "Urethritis, conjunctivitis and arthritis suggest reactive arthritis after chlamydia.",
        "Infant pneumonia with a staccato cough and no fever: think chlamydia."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines (current version)",
        "WHO guidance on trachoma elimination (SAFE strategy)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "listeria",
      "name": "Listeria monocytogenes",
      "group": "Gram-positive",
      "morphology": "Short Gram-positive bacilli with tumbling motility",
      "tagline": "Food-borne intracellular pathogen that grows at refrigerator temperature and causes meningitis and sepsis in neonates, pregnant women, older adults and immunocompromised patients.",
      "diseases": [
        "Febrile gastroenteritis",
        "Bacteraemia and sepsis",
        "Meningitis and meningoencephalitis",
        "Rhombencephalitis and brain abscess",
        "Pregnancy-associated infection (miscarriage, stillbirth)",
        "Neonatal listeriosis"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Short Gram-positive, non-spore-forming bacilli; facultative anaerobe and intracellular organism.\n• Tumbling motility at room temperature; umbrella-shaped growth in semi-solid agar at 25 degrees Celsius.\n• Catalase-positive, narrow zone of beta-haemolysis on blood agar, and esculin hydrolysis.\n• Grows at 4 degrees Celsius, so refrigeration does not stop it growing."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Internalins (InlA, InlB) bind E-cadherin and Met receptors to enter epithelial cells.\n• Listeriolysin O (LLO) lyses the phagosome and releases the bacteria into the cytoplasm.\n• ActA induces host actin polymerization, forming 'comet tails' that push the bacteria into neighbouring cells (cell-to-cell spread).\n• Phospholipases aid escape; the PrfA regulator controls these genes.\n• Cell-mediated immunity (T cells) controls infection, which explains the risk groups."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Found in soil, water, vegetation and animals; spread through contaminated food.\n• Common sources: unpasteurized milk and soft cheeses, deli meats, hot dogs, smoked fish, pre-cut melon, sprouts and other ready-to-eat foods.\n• Pregnant women are around ten times more likely to be infected than others.\n• Rare but serious: invasive disease has a case fatality of about 20%.\n• Outbreaks are often multi-state or national, and are traced by whole genome sequencing."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Healthy adults: febrile gastroenteritis within 24 hours of a large inoculum, usually self-limited.\n• Invasive disease: bacteraemia and meningitis or meningoencephalitis in neonates, adults over 50, pregnancy, transplant recipients, those on steroids or TNF inhibitors, diabetes, cirrhosis and HIV.\n• Brainstem infection (rhombencephalitis) with cranial nerve palsies and ataxia is characteristic; brain abscess occurs.\n• Pregnancy: mild flu-like illness with risk of miscarriage, stillbirth or preterm delivery.\n• Neonates: early-onset sepsis with granulomatosis infantiseptica, or late-onset meningitis."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Blood cultures and CSF culture are the mainstay; Gram stain of CSF is often negative or shows few organisms.\n• Can be mistaken for diphtheroid contaminants, so always identify the isolate.\n• Multiplex PCR panels detect Listeria in CSF but sensitivity is limited.\n• Stool culture is not routine except in outbreaks; maternal blood cultures in pregnancy with fever.\n• MRI for suspected rhombencephalitis or abscess."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Ampicillin (or penicillin G) is first-line; gentamicin is added for synergy in severe disease such as meningitis, endocarditis or bacteraemia in immunocompromised patients.\n• Penicillin allergy: trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole.\n• Meropenem is an alternative for meningitis.\n• Cephalosporins are not active, so empirical meningitis cover in neonates, adults over 50 and immunocompromised patients must include ampicillin.\n• Duration: at least 14 to 21 days for meningitis, and longer for brain abscess or rhombencephalitis.\n• Febrile gastroenteritis in healthy people usually needs no antibiotics."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Intrinsically resistant to all cephalosporins.\n• Also has reduced susceptibility to fluoroquinolones.\n• Resistance to ampicillin and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is rare."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Avoid unpasteurized milk and soft cheeses, and reheat deli meats and hot dogs until steaming, particularly in pregnancy and for at-risk groups.\n• Wash raw produce, keep ready-to-eat foods separate and avoid long storage, because the bacteria grow in the refrigerator.\n• Food industry controls and outbreak tracing.\n• No vaccine is available."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Cephalosporins do not cover Listeria: always add ampicillin to empirical meningitis therapy in neonates, older adults and the immunocompromised.",
        "Rhombencephalitis with fever suggests Listeria.",
        "Listeria grows in the refrigerator."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "Tunkel AR et al. IDSA guidelines for bacterial meningitis. Clin Infect Dis 2004",
        "Local public health food safety guidance"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b_pertussis",
      "name": "Bordetella pertussis",
      "group": "Gram-negative",
      "morphology": "Small Gram-negative coccobacilli, strictly aerobic",
      "tagline": "Highly contagious respiratory pathogen causing whooping cough, with prolonged paroxysmal cough; infants face the greatest risk of apnoea, pneumonia and death.",
      "diseases": [
        "Pertussis (whooping cough)",
        "Apnoea and cyanosis in young infants",
        "Secondary bacterial pneumonia",
        "Encephalopathy and seizures (severe cases)",
        "Prolonged cough in adolescents and adults"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Small, fastidious Gram-negative coccobacilli; strictly aerobic and non-motile.\n• Needs special media: Bordet-Gengou or Regan-Lowe charcoal agar; grows slowly (3 to 7 days).\n• Oxidase-positive; infects only humans and attaches to ciliated respiratory epithelium.\n• B. parapertussis causes a milder similar illness."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Pertussis toxin ADP-ribosylates Gi protein, raising cAMP; it causes lymphocytosis by blocking lymphocyte entry into tissues.\n• Filamentous haemagglutinin, pertactin and fimbriae mediate adhesion to cilia.\n• Adenylate cyclase toxin impairs phagocyte function.\n• Tracheal cytotoxin damages ciliated cells and stops mucociliary clearance, causing the cough.\n• Dermonecrotic toxin and lipooligosaccharide add local injury."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Humans are the only reservoir; spread by respiratory droplets, with a household attack rate of up to 80% in susceptible contacts.\n• Epidemic peaks every 3 to 5 years, with resurgence in many countries because immunity from acellular vaccines wanes over several years.\n• Adolescents and adults with mild or atypical disease are a major source for infants.\n• Highest mortality in infants under 3 months, and in places with low vaccine coverage."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Catarrhal stage (1 to 2 weeks): coryza, mild cough and low-grade fever, when the patient is most infectious.\n• Paroxysmal stage (2 to 6 weeks): bursts of rapid coughs followed by an inspiratory 'whoop', post-tussive vomiting, cyanosis and exhaustion, with the patient looking well between spells.\n• Convalescent stage (weeks to months): gradual decline of cough.\n• Infants may have apnoea, cyanosis or sudden death without a whoop.\n• Marked lymphocytosis in infants suggests pertussis, and complications include pneumonia, seizures and encephalopathy.\n• Adults: a cough lasting more than 2 weeks, often worse at night, with little fever."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• PCR on a nasopharyngeal swab or aspirate is the preferred test in the first 3 weeks of cough.\n• Culture on Regan-Lowe or Bordet-Gengou agar is specific but less sensitive, and needs early sampling.\n• Serology (IgG to pertussis toxin) can help in late presentation in adolescents and adults.\n• A full blood count showing high white cells with absolute lymphocytosis supports diagnosis in infants.\n• Notify public health and trace close contacts."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• A macrolide is first-line: azithromycin (preferred in young infants) or clarithromycin; erythromycin carries a risk of infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis in newborns.\n• Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is the alternative for macrolide-intolerant patients older than 2 months.\n• Antibiotics started in the catarrhal stage reduce symptoms; later they mainly stop spread.\n• Supportive care: oxygen, hydration, feeding support and monitoring for apnoea; admit infants under 6 months.\n• Cough suppressants, steroids and bronchodilators are not recommended.\n• Isolate for at least 5 days of effective antibiotics, and give close contacts post-exposure antibiotics, especially if there are infants or pregnant women at home."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Macrolide-resistant strains (23S rRNA mutations) are common in China and reported elsewhere.\n• Susceptibility testing is rarely done, so consider resistance with treatment failure.\n• Pertactin-deficient strains have emerged under acellular vaccine pressure."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• DTaP in the childhood series, with a booster (Tdap) in adolescence.\n• Tdap in every pregnancy (usually weeks 27 to 36) passes antibody to the newborn and is the best protection for infants.\n• Household and healthcare worker vaccination, and post-exposure prophylaxis for close contacts.\n• Whole-cell vaccines cause more side effects but give longer protection and are still used in many countries.\n• Natural infection and vaccine immunity both wane over time."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Adults with a cough over 2 weeks may be the source of infection for an infant.",
        "Whoop is often absent in infants, so look for apnoea and cyanosis.",
        "Antibiotics prevent spread, but only help symptoms if started early."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "CDC Pink Book: Pertussis chapter",
        "WHO position paper on pertussis vaccines"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_diphtheriae",
      "name": "Corynebacterium diphtheriae",
      "group": "Gram-positive",
      "morphology": "Club-shaped Gram-positive bacilli in 'Chinese letter' arrangements",
      "tagline": "Toxin-producing bacillus that causes respiratory diphtheria with an adherent pseudomembrane, plus myocarditis and neuropathy; vaccine-preventable but still causes outbreaks.",
      "diseases": [
        "Respiratory (faucial) diphtheria",
        "Laryngeal diphtheria with airway obstruction",
        "Cutaneous diphtheria",
        "Toxin-mediated myocarditis",
        "Diphtheritic neuropathy"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Club-shaped Gram-positive bacilli arranged in V and L shapes ('Chinese letters'); non-motile and non-spore-forming.\n• Metachromatic (volutin) granules stain with Albert or Loeffler methylene blue.\n• Grows on Loeffler serum slope and on tellurite media, producing black colonies on potassium tellurite agar.\n• Only strains carrying the tox gene (a lysogenic beta-prophage) make toxin; biotypes include gravis, mitis, intermedius and belfanti."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Diphtheria toxin: an AB exotoxin whose B subunit binds the HB-EGF receptor, and whose A subunit ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2 (EF-2), blocking protein synthesis and killing cells.\n• Toxin production is controlled by iron concentration, through the DtxR repressor.\n• The toxin causes local necrosis that forms the pseudomembrane, and spreads in blood to heart, nerves and kidneys.\n• Pili and other adhesins aid attachment to the throat."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• Spread by respiratory droplets and by contact with skin lesions; humans are the main reservoir.\n• Rare where vaccine coverage is high, but outbreaks occur when coverage falls (the former Soviet Union in the 1990s, and recent outbreaks in Yemen, Nigeria, Venezuela and Haiti).\n• Adults with waning immunity, travellers and unvaccinated children are at risk.\n• C. ulcerans, a zoonotic species carried by animals, can produce a diphtheria-like illness."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Incubation 2 to 5 days.\n• Sore throat, low fever and malaise, then a grey, adherent pseudomembrane on the tonsils and pharynx that bleeds when scraped.\n• 'Bull neck' from marked cervical lymphadenopathy and soft-tissue swelling, with foul breath.\n• Laryngeal involvement causes stridor and airway obstruction.\n• Myocarditis (typically at 1 to 2 weeks) causes arrhythmias and heart failure; neuropathy includes palatal palsy, cranial nerve palsies and later a peripheral polyneuropathy.\n• Cutaneous diphtheria: a chronic non-healing punched-out ulcer with a grey membrane, common in tropical regions."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Suspect from the clinical picture and do not wait for the laboratory; alert the lab and public health.\n• Swab from beneath the pseudomembrane for Gram stain, culture on Loeffler or tellurite media.\n• Confirm toxin production by the Elek immunodiffusion test or PCR for the tox gene.\n• ECG and troponin to monitor for myocarditis.\n• Culture contacts and treat carriers."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Diphtheria antitoxin (equine) neutralizes circulating toxin and should be given as soon as the diagnosis is suspected, after hypersensitivity testing, because it cannot reverse toxin already bound in cells.\n• Penicillin or a macrolide (erythromycin or azithromycin) is used to kill the organism and stop transmission.\n• Airway management, including intubation or tracheostomy for obstruction; bed rest and cardiac monitoring.\n• Respiratory droplet isolation until two negative cultures after antibiotics; contact precautions for cutaneous disease.\n• Vaccinate recovering patients, since natural infection does not reliably give immunity.\n• Close contacts: culture, antibiotic prophylaxis, booster vaccination, and surveillance for 7 days."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Generally susceptible to penicillin and macrolides.\n• Reduced penicillin susceptibility and macrolide resistance have been reported in some regions, so check susceptibility.\n• Resistance does not change the priority of antitoxin."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Diphtheria toxoid vaccine in DTaP or DTP (children) and Td or Tdap (adolescents and adults), with boosters every 10 years.\n• High vaccine coverage prevents disease and limits circulation.\n• Public health notification, contact tracing and outbreak response.\n• Booster vaccination for travellers to endemic areas."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "A grey adherent pseudomembrane that bleeds on scraping is classic for diphtheria.",
        "Give antitoxin on clinical suspicion, without waiting for culture.",
        "Diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A both inactivate EF-2 by ADP-ribosylation."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "CDC Pink Book: Diphtheria chapter",
        "WHO position paper on diphtheria vaccines"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b_anthracis",
      "name": "Bacillus anthracis",
      "group": "Gram-positive",
      "morphology": "Large Gram-positive spore-forming bacilli in chains ('boxcar' appearance)",
      "tagline": "Spore-forming zoonotic bacillus causing anthrax: cutaneous disease with a black eschar, and life-threatening inhalational, gastrointestinal and injection forms.",
      "diseases": [
        "Cutaneous anthrax (black eschar)",
        "Inhalational anthrax (haemorrhagic mediastinitis)",
        "Gastrointestinal anthrax",
        "Injection anthrax in people who inject drugs",
        "Anthrax meningitis (complication)"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Microbiology",
          "text": "• Large Gram-positive, aerobic, spore-forming bacilli, often in long chains with square ends ('boxcar' or 'bamboo' appearance); non-motile.\n• Non-haemolytic, grey 'medusa head' colonies on blood agar, and tolerant of aerobic culture.\n• Capsule is made of poly-D-glutamic acid, unusual among bacteria (it resists phagocytosis and is not a polysaccharide).\n• Spores survive in soil for decades and resist heat, drying and many disinfectants."
        },
        {
          "title": "Virulence Factors",
          "text": "• Plasmid pXO1 encodes the tripartite toxin: protective antigen (PA) binds host receptors and forms a pore for the other two components.\n• Edema factor (EF) is an adenylate cyclase that raises cAMP and causes oedema; lethal factor (LF) is a zinc metalloprotease that cleaves MAP kinase kinases and kills macrophages.\n• Plasmid pXO2 encodes the poly-D-glutamic acid capsule.\n• Spores are taken up by macrophages, germinate, and spread to lymph nodes, where bacteria multiply and release toxin."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epidemiology",
          "text": "• A zoonosis of herbivores (cattle, sheep, goats); humans are infected by contact with infected animals, hides, wool or meat, or by spore inhalation.\n• Endemic in parts of Africa, Asia, the Middle East and Central and South America; sporadic in other regions.\n• No sustained person-to-person spread.\n• Anthrax is a notifiable disease and a recognized bioterrorism agent (the 2001 letter attacks in the United States, and the 1979 Sverdlovsk release).\n• Injection anthrax has caused outbreaks among people who inject heroin in Europe."
        },
        {
          "title": "Clinical Features",
          "text": "• Cutaneous (about 95% of natural cases): a painless papule that turns into a vesicle and then a black necrotic eschar with surrounding oedema; usually heals over weeks, but can become systemic.\n• Inhalational: initial flu-like illness, then abrupt deterioration with respiratory failure, shock, widened mediastinum on chest X-ray and haemorrhagic pleural effusions, with high mortality.\n• Gastrointestinal: nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain and bloody diarrhoea after eating contaminated meat; oropharyngeal form with throat lesions.\n• Injection: severe soft tissue infection at the injection site with systemic spread.\n• Haemorrhagic meningitis can complicate any systemic form."
        },
        {
          "title": "Diagnosis",
          "text": "• Gram stain and culture of blood, vesicle fluid, CSF, sputum or tissue; warn the laboratory because it is a select agent.\n• Chest X-ray or CT showing mediastinal widening and pleural effusions supports inhalational anthrax.\n• PCR, immunohistochemistry and toxin assays are done in reference laboratories.\n• Treat on clinical suspicion, as delay is dangerous, and notify public health immediately.\n• Anthrax meningitis: consider lumbar puncture only when safe."
        },
        {
          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Cutaneous anthrax: oral ciprofloxacin or doxycycline for about 7 to 10 days in naturally acquired disease, and 60 days if the exposure was to aerosolized spores.\n• Systemic and inhalational anthrax: combination therapy with a bactericidal drug (ciprofloxacin or levofloxacin, or meropenem) plus a protein-synthesis inhibitor (linezolid or clindamycin) to reduce toxin production.\n• Antitoxins (raxibacumab, obiltoxaximab or anthrax immune globulin) are added for systemic disease.\n• Meningitis: use regimens with good CSF penetration (ciprofloxacin or meropenem plus linezolid).\n• Drain pleural effusions, and give intensive care support.\n• Post-exposure prophylaxis: ciprofloxacin or doxycycline for 60 days, with vaccination."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Naturally susceptible to penicillin, but produces inducible beta-lactamases and cephalosporinases, so penicillin and cephalosporins are not used alone.\n• Intrinsically resistant to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and many cephalosporins.\n• Fluoroquinolones, doxycycline, linezolid, clindamycin and carbapenems remain active.\n• Engineered resistant strains are a bioterrorism concern."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prevention",
          "text": "• Vaccination of animals in endemic regions, and safe disposal of carcasses (do not open them).\n• Anthrax vaccine adsorbed (AVA, BioThrax) and the newer Cyfendus are used for at-risk workers and for post-exposure use.\n• Protection of laboratory and veterinary workers; processing controls for imported hides and wool.\n• Public health tracing and decontamination after exposures."
        }
      ],
      "pearls": [
        "Painless black eschar with surrounding oedema: think cutaneous anthrax.",
        "Inhalational anthrax shows a widened mediastinum, not pneumonia infiltrates.",
        "Capsule of poly-D-glutamic acid is unique among bacteria."
      ],
      "references": [
        "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition",
        "CDC clinical guidance on anthrax (current version)",
        "WHO guidance on anthrax in humans and animals"
      ]
    }
  ]
